t  is as rapidly as possible and hopefully  as soon as it might be available for  distribution in clinics and use in the  clinics we hope that we will have the  capability of having and develop a  device that will be able to go home this  is essential for for this to be a  practical type of intervention so  certainly that's going to be our goal  and I think it's feasible what will the  spinal stimulation units cost there's a  big difference in the implantable system  and the transcutaneous system the  implantable system when by the time  you're combined all the hospital costs  you're maybe even in the range of a  hundred and fifty thousand dollars just  to get through the implant and and to  buy the device with the transcutaneous  stimulation we don't know how much that  device will cost but it may be somewhere  initially around ten to twenty thousand  dollars but with the possibility that  that could come down substantially  depending on many factors in the from  the commercial standpoint and when will  these devices be available to patients  well the device on one device that's  designed for pain is already available  if you get approval from FDA they use it  accordingly  there are a number of places that are  trying to develop smaller stimulators  for epidural stimulation and our lab is  one of those individuals individual labs  that's doing so and  our track a plan is to be able to have a  device that we think will be far  superior to what's presently available  available but it's going to take five to  six years and a lot more money to get  that to the point where it'd be  commercially available we think on the  other hand with the transcutaneous  stimulation that that device with a  acquiring adequate support this device  can be available within to the two and a  half years and we're obviously looking  forward to that day what other benefits  do you expect from spinal stimulation  with the therapy help reduce medical  costs we certainly think it's going to  reduce medical costs because it has  these multiple secondary effects you  might call secondary but the very  important effects related to autonomic  function we think that the the data in  the end we don't have these data now but  we want to get these data as rapidly as  possible we think the data are going to  show that by the time you consider all  the cost of the intervention itself  that's going to be far cheaper than not  having this intervention because of the  medical complications without having it  those medical complications the cost of  that over a period of lifetime is going  to be far greater than the initial cost  of the intervention that is our what we  are thinking at the time we don't have  the evidence to show that but that is  some evidence that we need to get can  the same device be used for hand  rehabilitation and leg rehabilitation  well not quite because you the functions  that you use with your hand and with  your legs will be different but both of  them are basing at the same time same  frame framework in that  we are trying to take advantage of the  activity dependent plasticity so we've  got to get this the cervical spinal  networks that are involved in a given  movement we have to have the apparatus  that's going to to enable that movement  to occur but we also want those devices  to have feedback to the subjects so they  will know how they're performing so  there's lots of room for improvement and  specialized devices and I think that's  going to be part of the new thinking of  how to fully take advantage of these  types of interventions so anyway I think  I'll stop there and thank you very much  for your attention and hope you enjoyed  this special virtual symposium in  neuroscience thank you  you
 

